                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="card" aria-labelledby="security-title">
    <h2 id="security-title">Security &amp; responsible AI</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Parameterized SQL only; validation in ViewModels and Application services; anti-forgery on POSTs.</li>
        <li>Cookie authentication with roles; login throttling (5 attempts / minute / IP); <code>/mcp</code> access key via <code>X-Api-Key</code>.</li>
        <li>Secrets in user-secrets locally and App Service settings in Azure—never in <code>appsettings.json</code> or Git.</li>
        <li>Offer Analyzer redacts PII before analysis; chat answers must use tool evidence; Demo fallback when no AI key.</li>
        <li>Sample data only; wording treats reports as impersonation of companies, not accusations that those companies ran the scam.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Limitations</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>SQLite on a single App Service instance (not a multi-region shared database).</li>
        <li>Training data is sample data for education; results are for class demos, not production threat intel.</li>
        <li>The language model can still misread tool output—UI and prompts push for grounded answers.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Fairness note</h3>
    <p>Reports describe scams that <em>impersonate</em> employers. JobLens must not present a company as the fraud actor when the data shows impersonation.</p>
</section>

